{
  "id": "shortest-path-in-dag",
  "questionId": "shortest-path-in-dag",
  "slug": "shortest-path-in-dag",
  "title": "Shortest Path in a DAG",
  "difficulty": "Medium",
  "category": "Topological sort",
  "patterns": [
    "topological sort",
    "graph",
    "dag",
    "shortest path"
  ],
  "lists": [],
  "hints": [
    "in a general graph you might reach for Dijkstra, but this graph has no cycles - is there a cheaper ordering that guarantees every predecessor of a node is finalized before you process it?",
    "get a topological order of the DAG first",
    "initialize dist[src] = 0 and everything else to infinity, then walk the nodes in topological order",
    "for each node u you process, relax every outgoing edge (u, v, w): if dist[u] + w improves dist[v], update it",
    "after processing all nodes in topo order, any node still at the sentinel infinity value was never reached - report it as -1"
  ],
  "commonMistakes": [
    "relaxing edges in an order that isn't a valid topological order, which can under-update a node's distance before all of its cheaper incoming paths have been considered",
    "forgetting to convert the sentinel 'infinity' value to -1 for unreachable nodes before returning",
    "initializing dist[src] to something other than 0, or leaving other nodes at 0 instead of infinity, causing false 'reachable' results"
  ],
  "content": {
    "problemStatement": "You're given a directed acyclic graph (DAG) with `n` nodes, labeled 0 to\nn - 1, and a list of weighted edges, where each edge `[u, v, w]` means\nthere is an edge from node `u` to node `v` with a non-negative weight\n`w`. You're also given a source node `src`.\n\n**Return an array `dist` of length n, where `dist[i]` is the length of the shortest path from `src` to node i, the sum of edge weights along the path.**\n\nIf node i is unreachable from src, dist[i] should be -1. dist[src] is always 0.\n",
    "constraints": [
      "1 <= n <= 10^4",
      "0 <= edges.length <= 10^5",
      "edges[i].length == 3, with 0 <= u, v < n and 0 <= w <= 10^5",
      "the graph is guaranteed to have no cycles",
      "0 <= src < n"
    ],
    "notes": [
      "src has no outgoing edges at all - only itself is reachable",
      "a node reachable through multiple paths with different total weights - the minimum must be chosen",
      "a node completely disconnected from src - its distance is -1",
      "a single node graph with no edges at all"
    ],
    "inputFormat": "Arguments passed into solution function",
    "outputFormat": "Return output matching method signature",
    "sampleTestCases": [
      {
        "id": "sample_1",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 4, edges = [[0, 1, 2], [0, 2, 4], [1, 2, 1], [1, 3, 7], [2, 3, 3]], src = 0",
        "output": "[0, 2, 3, 6]",
        "expectedOutput": "[0, 2, 3, 6]",
        "explanation": "the shortest way to node 2 is 0 -> 1 -> 2 (cost 3), beating the direct edge 0 -> 2 (cost 4); the shortest way to node 3 is 0 -> 1 -> 2 -> 3 (cost 6)"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_2",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 3, edges = [[0, 1, 5]], src = 2",
        "output": "[-1, -1, 0]",
        "expectedOutput": "[-1, -1, 0]",
        "explanation": "node 2 has no outgoing edges and nothing reaches it, so nodes 0 and 1 stay unreachable from it"
      },
      {
        "id": "sample_3",
        "type": "reg",
        "input": "n = 5, edges = [[0, 1, 1], [1, 2, 1], [2, 3, 1], [0, 4, 10]], src = 0",
        "output": "[0, 1, 2, 3, 10]",
        "expectedOutput": "[0, 1, 2, 3, 10]",
        "explanation": "node 4 is only reachable through the direct edge of weight 10, since there's no other route to it"
      }
    ],
